//! Finite, asset-backed heightfield terrain shared by rendering and physics.
//!
//! Heightmaps should be 16-bit grayscale PNGs. Black maps to the declared
//! minimum height and white maps to the maximum height.

/// An immutable terrain descriptor.
type t = host

/// One ground material for `Terrain.materials`: a tiled albedo.
type layer = host

/// Create a terrain centered on the origin and spanning `width` by `depth` in XZ.
///
/// Prefer a square map of 2ⁿ+1 samples per side (65, 129, …, 1025, 2049),
/// within the GPU's texture size limit: every terrain vertex then sits exactly
/// on a sample, so the GPU reads one height per sample instead of four. Other
/// sizes render the same surface, only more slowly.
let heightmap : (Asset.Texture, float, float, float, float) => t

/// Set the maximum projected vertex spacing in pixels; lower is more detailed.
///
/// The default is 2 pixels. Under `Frame.withFog` without a skybox, the
/// allowance grows as fog hides the terrain (divided by the fog's visibility
/// at each patch, at most 4×). The terrain is last for piping.
let maxPixelError : (float, t) => t

/// Set the basic lit terrain color; the terrain is last for piping.
///
/// This is the ALTERNATIVE to `Terrain.layered`, not a stage before it: it
/// clears any layers, so whichever of the two comes last in a pipeline wins
/// and the earlier one is simply dead. Pick one.
let color : (Color.t, t) => t

/// Blend lowland, highland, rock, and snow colors by height and slope.
///
/// `snowHeight` is in terrain-local world units. The terrain is last for
/// piping.
let layered : (Color.t, Color.t, Color.t, Color.t, float, t) => t

/// Dress the `layered` bands with detail maps.
///
/// Each map supplies STRUCTURE, not color: it is divided by its own average, so
/// it adds surface detail to the band's `layered` color without repainting it.
/// (A photographic ground albedo averages brown; used directly it would turn a
/// green hillside to dirt.) The maps blend by the same height and slope weights
/// as the colors, so texturing changes what a band looks like, not where it
/// falls. `tileSize` is the world-unit span of one tile; each map is sampled at
/// two scales to hide the repeat, and detail fades out with distance. Requires
/// `layered`. The terrain is last for piping.
let textured : (Asset.Texture, Asset.Texture, Asset.Texture, Asset.Texture, float, t) => t

/// Shade the terrain from two baked colormaps instead of lighting it.
///
/// `day` and `night` are color images laid once over the whole terrain, the
/// same way as the heightmap, with lighting, occlusion and banding already
/// painted in. The terrain is then UNLIT: frame lights do not reach it; only
/// fog applies. `blend` cross-fades the maps, clamped to 0..1: 0 is `day` only
/// and 1 is `night` only, and only those two values read a single map. Both
/// maps stay on the GPU while the terrain draws, so changing `blend` uploads
/// nothing.
///
/// Takes precedence over `color`, `layered` and `textured`, which render only
/// until both maps have streamed in (and in the normals/tangents debug views).
/// The terrain is last for piping.
let colormap : (Asset.Texture, Asset.Texture, float, t) => t

/// Dress a `colormap` with one tiled detail map near the camera.
///
/// Like `textured`, the map supplies STRUCTURE, not color: it is divided by its
/// own average, so it roughens the colormap without tinting it. It is full
/// strength near the camera and fades to nothing by `fadeEnd` world units; past
/// that, pixels read the colormap alone. `tileSize` is the world-unit span of
/// one tile. Requires `colormap`. The terrain is last for piping.
let colormapDetail : (Asset.Texture, float, float, t) => t

/// Let a flashlight light a `colormap` terrain, using this normal map.
///
/// The colormap terrain is unlit, with one exception: the frame's first
/// `Light.spot` that is not `Light.baked` adds its light where its cone and
/// range reach the ground, with the same falloff as on lit surfaces and no
/// shadows. Every other light stays painted into the maps. Pixels outside the
/// beam pay only a cone test; inside it, the terrain fetches this map. The
/// beam lights the `day` map's color, at any `blend`: the night map is the
/// same ground already darkened, so a beam on it would read dim and blue. The
/// day map's baked sun comes with it, so slopes that faced the sun read
/// brighter under the beam than slopes that faced away.
///
/// The map is laid over the terrain once, like the colormaps, and stores
/// terrain-local unit normals as `xyz × 0.5 + 0.5` (+Y up, so flat ground is
/// `(128, 255, 128)`). Bake it for the same width, depth and height range the
/// heightmap is given. Both it and the day map are read at full resolution
/// (no mips) inside the beam, which assumes a texel is wider than a pixel at
/// the beam's range: true of a terrain-wide map (1024² over 4 km is 3.9 m a
/// texel). Without a normal map the flashlight does not reach the
/// terrain. Ignored without `colormap`. The terrain is last for piping.
let normalMap : (Asset.Texture, t) => t

/// A ground material for `Terrain.materials`: an albedo map tiled every
/// `tileSize` world units.
let layer : (Asset.Texture, float) => layer

/// Dress a `colormap` with up to four ground materials, painted by a splat map.
///
/// `splat` is laid once over the terrain, like the colormaps. Its R, G, B and
/// A channels weight `layers` 0 to 3 (stored linear, not as sRGB color); where
/// they sum below 1, the detail fades toward the bare colormap. Near the
/// camera each pixel blends its two heaviest layers, each sampled at two
/// scales to hide the repeat, and the result fades out by `fadeEnd` world
/// units. Past that, pixels read the colormap alone, at no extra cost.
///
/// A layer adds BRIGHTNESS, not color: each sample's luminance is divided by
/// the layer's mean luminance, so the colormap keeps its hue. All of a
/// terrain's albedo maps must be the same size. Until every layer has loaded,
/// `colormapDetail` (if any) shows instead; `Asset.whilePending` stand-ins
/// are not used for layers. Requires `colormap`. The terrain is last for
/// piping.
let materials : (Asset.Texture, List<layer>, float, t) => t

/// Add camera-local GPU-instanced grass clusters.
///
/// `spacing`, `distance`, and `bladeHeight` are terrain-local world units.
/// Grass is suppressed on steep, low-lying, and snowy samples. The terrain is
/// last for piping.
let grass : (float, float, float, Color.t, t) => t
